ex:StorageHost_Define_the_name_of_the_host_the_database_is_running_where_we_are_going_to_store_the_data_This_can_be_the_host_on_which_slurmdbd_executes_but_for_larger_systems_we_recommend_keeping_the_database_on_a_separate_machine a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StorageHost: Define the name of the host the database is running where we are going to store the data. This can be the host on which slurmdbd executes, but for larger systems, we recommend keeping the database on a separate machine." .

ex:StorageLoc_Specifies_the_name_of_the_database_where_accounting_records_are_written_For_databases_the_default_database_is_slurm_acct_db_Note_the_name_can_not_have_a_in_it_or_the_default_will_be_used a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StorageLoc: Specifies the name of the database where accounting records are written. For databases the default database is slurm_acct_db. Note the name can not have a '/' in it or the default will be used." .

ex:StoragePass_Define_the_password_used_to_gain_access_to_the_database_to_store_the_job_accounting_data a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StoragePass: Define the password used to gain access to the database to store the job accounting data." .

ex:StoragePort_Define_the_port_on_which_the_database_is_listening a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StoragePort: Define the port on which the database is listening." .

ex:StorageType_Define_the_accounting_storage_mechanism_The_only_acceptable_value_at_present_is_accounting_storage_mysql_The_value_accounting_storage_mysql_indicates_that_accounting_records_should_be_written_to_a_MySQL_or_MariaDB_database_specified_by_the_StorageLoc_parameter_This_value_must_be_specified a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StorageType: Define the accounting storage mechanism. The only acceptable value at present is \"accounting_storage/mysql\". The value \"accounting_storage/mysql\" indicates that accounting records should be written to a MySQL or MariaDB database specified by the StorageLoc parameter. This value must be specified." .

ex:StorageUser_Define_the_name_of_the_user_we_are_going_to_connect_to_the_database_with_to_store_the_job_accounting_data a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "StorageUser: Define the name of the user we are going to connect to the database with to store the job accounting data." .

ex:Storage_Backup_Host a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Storage Backup Host" .

ex:Storing_the_accounting_information_into_text_files_is_very_simple_Just_configure_the_appropriate_plugin_e_g_JobCompType_jobcomp_filetxt_and_then_specify_the_pathname_of_the_file_e_g_JobCompLoc_var_log_slurm_job_completions_Use_the_logrotate_or_similar_tool_to_prevent_the_log_files_from_getting_too_large_Send_a_SIGUSR2_signal_to_the_slurmctld_daemon_after_moving_the_files_but_before_compressing_them_so_that_new_log_files_will_be_created a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Storing the accounting information into text files is very simple. Just configure the appropriate plugin (e.g. JobCompType=jobcomp/filetxt) and then specify the pathname of the file (e.g. JobCompLoc=/var/log/slurm/job_completions). Use the logrotate or similar tool to prevent the log files from getting too large. Send a SIGUSR2 signal to the slurmctld daemon after moving the files, but before compressing them so that new log files will be created." .

ex:Storing_the_data_directly_into_a_database_from_Slurm_may_seem_attractive_but_it_requires_the_availability_of_user_name_and_password_data_not_only_for_the_Slurm_control_daemon_slurmctld_but_also_for_user_commands_which_need_to_access_the_data_sacct_sreport_and_sacctmgr_Making_potentially_sensitive_information_available_to_all_users_makes_database_security_more_difficult_to_provide_Sending_the_data_through_an_intermediate_daemon_can_provide_better_security_and_performance_through_caching_data_SlurmDBD_Slurm_Database_Daemon_provides_such_services_SlurmDBD_is_written_in_C_multi_threaded_secure_and_fast_The_configuration_required_to_use_SlurmDBD_will_be_described_below_Storing_information_directly_into_a_database_would_be_similar a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Storing the data directly into a database from Slurm may seem attractive, but it requires the availability of user name and password data not only for the Slurm control daemon (slurmctld), but also for user commands which need to access the data (sacct, sreport, and sacctmgr). Making potentially sensitive information available to all users makes database security more difficult to provide. Sending the data through an intermediate daemon can provide better security and performance (through caching data). SlurmDBD (Slurm Database Daemon) provides such services. SlurmDBD is written in C, multi-threaded, secure and fast. The configuration required to use SlurmDBD will be described below. Storing information directly into a database would be similar." .

